/// TracingAnalyticsQuestionBank — 72 questions (62 single-select, 10 multi-select)
import Foundation

enum TracingAnalyticsQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A consulting team is scoping an Agentforce deployment and wants to know why traditional QA testing isn't sufficient for validating an AI agent before launch. Which best explains the underlying reason? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "AI agents are probabilistic, so identical inputs can produce varying outputs depending on phrasing and context, unlike deterministic code paths that unit tests reliably cover."), ("B", "AI agents cannot be tested in sandbox environments at all, so all validation must occur after go-live using real production traffic and users."), ("C", "AI agents require twice as much Apex test coverage as standard Salesforce automation, so QA teams need additional headcount to reach the required percentage."), ("D", "AI agents only fail when integrations are misconfigured, so testing conversational flows separately from backend actions is unnecessary and wastes QA effort.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Language model outputs vary with phrasing, history, and non-deterministic model behavior, so a handful of successful test sessions cannot guarantee behavior across the full diversity of real user phrasing. B is incorrect: Sandbox preview tooling exists specifically to validate agent behavior before production release. C is incorrect: Apex test coverage requirements apply to deployed code, not conversational agent behavior. D is incorrect: Conversational routing and instruction quality fail independently of backend integrations."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "An admin runs a query against a knowledge-related object and receives an INVALID_TYPE error. What does this most likely indicate, and what should the admin check first? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The org has exceeded its API request limit for the day, so the admin should wait until the daily limit resets before retrying the query."), ("B", "Data Cloud has not been provisioned in the org, so the admin should verify Data Cloud setup and CRM Connector status before configuring any observability features."), ("C", "The user's profile lacks Read access on the object, so the admin should assign a permission set granting object-level Read access and retry the query."), ("D", "The query used an invalid relationship field, so the admin should consult the schema builder to find the correct child relationship name.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Data Cloud-backed DMOs and knowledge objects only exist once Data Cloud is provisioned; INVALID_TYPE is the classic signal the platform layer isn't active. A is incorrect: API limit exhaustion produces a REQUEST_LIMIT_EXCEEDED error, a distinct error type. C is incorrect: A permissions issue typically returns an empty result set or access error, not INVALID_TYPE. D is incorrect: An invalid relationship name produces a different error referencing the relationship, not INVALID_TYPE."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "A back-end tracing feature that captures LLM calls, Flow runs, and Apex invocations for every Agentforce action is not showing any data even though the agent has been live for a month. What is the most likely cause? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The feature requires a paid add-on license that must be purchased separately before any span data begins populating in the underlying data model."), ("B", "The feature only captures data during business hours in the org's default time zone, so after-hours sessions are silently dropped from the trace store."), ("C", "The feature only activates automatically once monthly session volume exceeds ten thousand conversations, so smaller deployments never generate visible span data."), ("D", "The feature must be explicitly enabled via a Setup toggle, and there is no retroactive backfill, so all prior sessions before enabling it remain invisible.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Service-level platform tracing is off by default and must be manually enabled, with no backfill of historical sessions. A is incorrect: The feature is a configuration toggle, not a separately licensed add-on. B is incorrect: The feature captures spans continuously regardless of time of day. C is incorrect: There is no volume-based auto-activation threshold; it's controlled by an explicit toggle."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "Which permission is specifically required for a user to author agents, run preview sessions, and publish agents through the CLI and Agentforce Studio? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "CopilotSalesforceAdmin"), ("B", "AgentforceServiceAgentUser"), ("C", "DataCloudUser"), ("D", "GenieUserEnhancedSecurity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This permission set grants access to Agentforce Studio and the agent authoring/publishing APIs. B is incorrect: This is a runtime execution identity for service agents, not an authoring permission. C is incorrect: This grants Data Cloud query access for a runtime user, not authoring/publishing capability. D is incorrect: This is a Data Cloud query-access variant for knowledge-grounded runtime queries, not Studio access."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "A team notices that queries against Data Cloud trace tables return zero rows for a user, with no error message at all. What is the most likely explanation? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "DMO queries silently fail when more than three WHERE clauses are combined, so the query must be simplified to two or fewer filter conditions."), ("B", "The org has reached its monthly Data Cloud row-processing limit, which causes all subsequent queries that month to return empty result sets without warning."), ("C", "The user is missing the Data Cloud Data Access permission, and missing DMO permissions typically return empty results rather than explicit access-denied errors."), ("D", "The DMO tables have a hard 24-hour data retention window, so any query run more than a day after session activity will always return zero rows.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Missing DMO read access is a common cause of \"no data\" since Salesforce returns an empty result set rather than a permission error. A is incorrect: SOQL against DMOs does not impose an undocumented WHERE-clause limit. B is incorrect: Reaching a processing limit produces a distinct governor-style error. D is incorrect: DMO retention windows are configurable and typically span well beyond 24 hours."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "Which statement best describes the relationship between usage/adoption analytics, deep session tracing, and real-time health monitoring in an observability strategy? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "All three capabilities query the exact same underlying data model, so any one of them alone provides complete coverage without needing the other two."), ("B", "Analytics dashboards are only useful before launch, while tracing and health monitoring are the only tools relevant to a live production agent."), ("C", "Health monitoring replaces the need for session-level tracing once an agent has been in production for more than 90 days without major incidents."), ("D", "Analytics answers whether the agent is being used, tracing answers why a specific session behaved a certain way, and health monitoring answers whether the platform is safe and healthy right now.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Each pillar maps to a distinct enterprise question, and all three are needed together for full-picture observability. A is incorrect: The three draw on related but distinct data sources and granularities. B is incorrect: Pre-launch analytics remain valuable post-launch for tracking adoption trends. C is incorrect: Health monitoring detects systemic issues but can't explain a specific conversation's root cause."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "Two weeks after launch, an agent shows a high percentage of sessions resolved without human escalation, but customer complaints about unresolved issues haven't dropped. What should the team investigate first? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Whether the dashboard's session duration panel is using the correct time zone configuration, since incorrect time zones could inflate the reported deflection percentage."), ("B", "Whether the customer complaint intake form has a data entry bug, since complaint volume is unrelated to how deflection is measured on the dashboard."), ("C", "Whether the underlying LLM model version was recently upgraded, since newer model versions are known to reduce the accuracy of escalation-rate reporting entirely."), ("D", "Whether \"resolved\" sessions actually contain a generic fallback message rather than genuine help, since sessions ending without escalation are still counted as deflected even when nothing was actually solved.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A session is marked deflected whenever it doesn't escalate, even if it only produced a generic failure message, masking genuine failures. A is incorrect: A time zone issue would skew timestamps, not the deflection calculation itself. B is incorrect: Ignoring the complaint pattern dismisses the strongest available signal. C is incorrect: Model version changes affect quality, not how the metric is calculated."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "Within the first two weeks after a new agent launches, which metric should be monitored most closely as the earliest indicator of unmet user intents? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Daily active user count, because a decline in unique users is the most sensitive and immediate signal of a specific instruction gap."), ("B", "Escalation rate, because it is the first real-world signal that a category of user need isn't handled, appearing before users start complaining or abandoning the channel."), ("C", "Average session duration, because any session lasting longer than sixty seconds automatically indicates a routing or grounding failure."), ("D", "Token usage per session, because rising token counts always indicate that the agent is misrouting conversations to the wrong subagent.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Test utterances can't fully represent real phrasing diversity, so escalation rate is the fastest real-world feedback signal. A is incorrect: DAU decline is a lagging, diffuse signal shaped by many factors. C is incorrect: Session duration varies naturally with task complexity; there's no fixed failure threshold. D is incorrect: Token usage reflects conversation complexity, not routing correctness."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A support team wants an automated way to detect when an agent asks the same clarifying question repeatedly across multiple turns without ever resolving it. Which analysis approach is best suited to this? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "An LLM-as-a-Judge style custom evaluation that scans conversation logs for repeated instruction-execution patterns indicating an unresolved loop."), ("B", "Disabling grounding checks temporarily, since grounding checks are the most common root cause of an agent repeating the same question multiple times."), ("C", "Increasing the safety score threshold on agent responses, since higher safety thresholds automatically detect and interrupt repetitive behavioral loops."), ("D", "Reviewing the consumption dashboard's token breakdown by user, since users experiencing loops always show a token usage pattern below the organizational average.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Semantic pattern detection across logs is purpose-built for spotting behavioral loops that no single trace reveals alone. B is incorrect: Grounding checks don't cause or diagnose repetitive questioning. C is incorrect: Safety scoring evaluates harmfulness, not conversational repetition. D is incorrect: Token usage patterns aren't a reliable proxy for looped conversations."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "Which alerting condition would most directly indicate a systemic instruction or routing failure requiring immediate trace review, rather than an isolated one-off issue? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "A single user reporting that one response felt unhelpful during a routine feedback survey."), ("B", "More than ten fallback responses occurring within a five-minute window."), ("C", "One session exceeding the average session duration by a small margin during a low-traffic period."), ("D", "A single Apex action returning zero rows for one specific account lookup on one occasion.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A concentrated burst of fallbacks in a short window signals a pattern affecting many users, the hallmark of a systemic problem. A is incorrect: A single subjective report doesn't by itself indicate a systemic issue. C is incorrect: A minor duration deviation during low traffic is normal variance. D is incorrect: One isolated zero-row result is consistent with a legitimate \"no data\" case."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "A team wants to prevent personally identifiable information from reaching an external LLM provider and wants every prompt and response logged for compliance. Which platform capability provides this governance layer? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The Einstein Trust Layer, which masks PII, scores response safety, logs prompts and responses for audit, and can trigger fail-safe retries or fallback responses."), ("B", "The Agent Platform Tracing span attributes, which automatically strip PII from all telemetry data before persisting it into Data Cloud."), ("C", "The Data Cloud STDM tables, which are purpose-built to permanently redact personal data from any object before it is written to any DMO."), ("D", "The Consumption Analytics Dashboard, which enforces PII masking policies across every Prompt Template invocation triggered by an agent.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "PII masking, safety scoring, audit logging, and fail-safe routing are the defining capabilities of the platform's AI governance layer. B is incorrect: Platform Tracing spans capture execution telemetry, not PII stripping. C is incorrect: STDM tables store session/interaction data for analytics, not redaction. D is incorrect: The consumption dashboard reports token/credit usage, not PII policy."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "A finance-services agent's escalation rate spikes only during two specific recurring windows each week, and a health alert fires each time. What is the most effective first diagnostic step? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Increase the LLM model's temperature setting, since higher temperature settings are the standard fix for recurring, time-boxed escalation spikes."), ("B", "Pull trace files from sessions within the alert window and look for a shared step-level error pattern, such as a specific action consistently failing."), ("C", "Immediately disable the agent entirely until the next scheduled maintenance window, since repeating alert patterns always indicate an unrecoverable platform outage."), ("D", "Wait for the next monthly Consumption Insights report, since escalation spikes tied to a weekly schedule are best diagnosed only in aggregate monthly data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Following the alert to its time window and drilling into traces is the direct path to a shared root cause like a recurring backend timeout. A is incorrect: Temperature affects response variability, not backend timeouts. C is incorrect: A recurring predictable pattern is more likely a fixable dependency issue than an outage. D is incorrect: Waiting delays root-cause discovery unnecessarily."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "A developer wants to test a new agent's routing logic before the backing Apex actions have been built. Which testing mode is most appropriate for this stage? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Production monitoring dashboards, since routing logic can only be reliably validated using real user traffic rather than any preview tooling."), ("B", "Live preview mode, since only real action execution can validate routing decisions and instruction compilation at any stage of development."), ("C", "Platform Tracing span review, since inspecting backend execution spans is the only way to confirm which subagent a conversation was routed to."), ("D", "Simulated preview mode, where the LLM generates fake action outputs, since the backing implementation doesn't yet exist to call.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Simulated mode lets the LLM generate placeholder outputs so routing/instruction logic can be validated before real code exists. A is incorrect: Testing against live production traffic before the backend exists risks real user impact. B is incorrect: Live mode requires real actions to exist and execute. C is incorrect: With no backing implementation, there's nothing for Platform Tracing to trace."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A grounding check on an agent's response repeatedly fails during simulated preview testing, but the developer suspects it's a false positive. What should they do next? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Add more few-shot examples to the system prompt, since grounding failures are fixed exclusively through expanded prompt engineering rather than mode changes."), ("B", "Delete the failing trace file, since trace files replay stale results and regenerating a fresh session ID always clears a grounding false positive."), ("C", "Switch to live preview mode, since the LLM's fake action outputs generated during simulation may not match real data patterns and can cause misleading grounding failures."), ("D", "Lower the safety score threshold, since safety scoring and grounding checks are the same mechanism and adjusting one resolves failures in the other.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Grounding compares the response against actual action output, and simulated mode's fabricated outputs don't reflect real data patterns. A is incorrect: This symptom is tied to simulated fake outputs, not prompt content. B is incorrect: Trace files reflect what actually happened; deleting one doesn't change behavior. D is incorrect: Safety scoring and grounding are distinct checks."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "Within a per-turn execution trace, which step type would show the exact input the user typed to trigger that turn? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "SessionInitialStateStep"), ("B", "TransitionStep"), ("C", "NodeEntryStateStep"), ("D", "UserInputStep")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "This step type is specifically defined to capture the literal utterance that initiated the turn. A is incorrect: This step captures variable values and context at the start of the turn, not the user's text. B is incorrect: This step records subagent routing changes, not the user's input text. C is incorrect: This step shows which subagent is executing and its state, not the user's utterance."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "Which trace step, if missing for an action the agent should have been able to call, indicates the action was never even visible to the LLM for that reasoning cycle? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "EnabledToolsStep"), ("B", "ReasoningStep"), ("C", "VariableUpdateStep"), ("D", "PlannerResponseStep")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This step lists exactly which actions were exposed to the LLM; absence means it was structurally unavailable. B is incorrect: This captures the grounding assessment result, not tool visibility. C is incorrect: This records a variable's old/new value, unrelated to tool visibility. D is incorrect: This captures the final response and safety score, not action availability."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A trace shows a chat response claiming a record was updated, but the developer cannot find the record change reflected anywhere. What should they check in the trace, and what does its absence mean? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Whether the PlannerResponseStep's safety score exceeds 0.9; a lower score always means the underlying action silently failed without logging an error."), ("B", "Whether a FunctionStep for the update action exists; if it doesn't, the action never actually executed regardless of what the response text claims."), ("C", "Whether the SessionInitialStateStep lists the correct session ID; a mismatched session ID is the only possible explanation for an unconfirmed update claim."), ("D", "Whether the TransitionStep shows a handoff to a different subagent; any subagent transition guarantees that the prior action's database write was rolled back.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The LLM can fabricate confident success claims without invoking a real action; FunctionStep presence is the authoritative evidence. A is incorrect: Safety score measures harmfulness, not action execution. C is incorrect: A session ID mismatch is an unrelated logging issue. D is incorrect: A transition doesn't inherently roll back a prior write."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "An instruction tells the agent to \"respond using the exact date and temperature values returned by the action; do not paraphrase or round.\" What quality problem is this instruction pattern designed to prevent? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Credit overconsumption caused by an agent invoking the same Flow action multiple times within a single reasoning cycle."), ("B", "Safety score degradation caused by an agent using informal language patterns instead of formal business terminology."), ("C", "Routing failures caused by ambiguous subagent descriptions that overlap across multiple topics handled by the platform."), ("D", "Grounding failures caused by the agent embellishing, rounding, or loosely paraphrasing action output instead of quoting it verbatim.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Grounding checks compare a response against exact action output; common failures include rounding and loose paraphrasing. A is incorrect: Repeated invocation is a cost issue unrelated to value precision. B is incorrect: Safety scoring evaluates harmfulness, not numeric precision. C is incorrect: Routing ambiguity is fixed through clearer descriptions, not verbatim-value instructions."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Before testing a new action within the full multi-subagent agent flow, what is the recommended first validation step? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Skip functional testing altogether and rely solely on the safety score returned in the PlannerResponseStep to validate the action's correctness."), ("B", "Test the backing Flow or Prompt Template in isolation using Prompt Builder, since the full agent path introduces routing and state variables as confounding factors."), ("C", "Run the action exclusively in production once traffic volume is high enough to statistically validate its behavior across many real conversations."), ("D", "Enable live preview mode across the entire agent immediately, since isolated component testing before full-flow testing is not a recognized best practice.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Isolating the implementation removes routing decisions and non-determinism as confounding variables. A is incorrect: Safety score doesn't reflect functional correctness. C is incorrect: Validating in live production risks real user impact. D is incorrect: Isolated testing before full-path testing is a widely recommended practice."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "Which utterance is best suited for realistic agent testing, most closely matching how actual users phrase requests? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "\"I've been thinking about it and I want to cancel my subscription.\""), ("B", "\"cancel\""), ("C", "\"reset password\""), ("D", "\"order status\"")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Natural, conversational phrasing exercises intent recognition the way real users actually communicate. B is incorrect: A single keyword doesn't reflect natural phrasing and can mask routing weaknesses. C is incorrect: A bare command-style phrase doesn't exercise conversational interpretation. D is incorrect: A short noun-phrase skips the natural framing real users provide."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "In a multi-subagent architecture, what should be verified to confirm that a variable set by the router subagent correctly reaches a subagent it hands off to? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "That the variable appears with the expected value in the receiving subagent's NodeEntryStateStep."), ("B", "That the PlannerResponseStep's safety score for the router subagent exceeds the safety score of the receiving subagent."), ("C", "That the EnabledToolsStep for the router lists every action available to the receiving subagent as well."), ("D", "That the variable appears in the router's own SessionInitialStateStep before any handoff occurs at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Confirming the variable's value in the receiving subagent's entry-state snapshot is the direct evidence of correct propagation. B is incorrect: Comparing safety scores has no bearing on variable state sync. C is incorrect: Tool visibility lists don't confirm variable state sync. D is incorrect: This confirms the variable existed but not that it reached the subagent."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "A trace tree shows a step labeled with a \"create record\" style operation name, but its span attributes show a database read operation with zero rows affected. What does this reveal about interpreting trace data? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The span is corrupted and should be discarded, since operation names are always a perfectly reliable indicator of the true underlying database action performed."), ("B", "The action must have thrown an unhandled exception, since any span attribute mismatch with its operation name always indicates an unrecoverable Apex runtime error."), ("C", "The operation name on a span doesn't always describe what the operation actually did; span attributes are needed to confirm the real underlying behavior."), ("D", "The parent span's duration is invalid, since an operation-name mismatch always propagates and invalidates duration measurements throughout the entire trace tree.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Span attributes can reveal that a labeled operation name doesn't match real behavior, critical for correctly diagnosing write vs. lookup issues. A is incorrect: The scenario shows operation names can be misleading, contradicting this claim. B is incorrect: A mismatch doesn't itself indicate an exception; this shows a successful zero-row read. D is incorrect: Duration measurements are independent of naming conventions."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "Which field on a back-end execution span is used to reconstruct the parent-child hierarchy of a trace tree? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The field storing the span's status code, since only spans marked as errors are eligible to have child spans beneath them."), ("B", "The field storing the service name that generated the span, since spans sharing the same service name are grouped as siblings under a shared parent."), ("C", "The field storing the parent span's identifier, where a null or all-zero value marks that span as the root of the tree."), ("D", "The field storing the operation's duration in milliseconds, since spans with the shortest duration are always treated as the tree's root.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Matching each span's parent-identifier field to another span's own identifier reconstructs the hierarchy client-side. A is incorrect: Both OK and ERROR spans can have children. B is incorrect: Service name reflects the generating system, not tree position. D is incorrect: Duration has no bearing on hierarchy position."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "A customer complains an agent \"feels slow\" but no specific failing session has been reported. What is the recommended first diagnostic query approach? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Query the Consumption Analytics Dashboard's Daily Active Users panel, since user growth trends are the primary indicator of systemic latency problems."), ("B", "Query the Einstein Trust Layer safety score distribution, since safety score directly correlates with end-to-end response latency in nearly all cases."), ("C", "Query average and max duration grouped by operation type across recent spans to identify which operation types are consistently the bottleneck."), ("D", "Query GenAIFeedback records exclusively, since user thumbs-down ratings always specify the exact operation type responsible for perceived slowness.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Aggregating duration by operation type is the standard way to surface consistently slow steps without a specific session to investigate. A is incorrect: Active user counts reflect adoption, not per-operation latency. B is incorrect: Safety score reflects harmfulness, unrelated to latency. D is incorrect: Feedback captures sentiment, not structured operation-level data."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "A performance query reveals that one action's average duration is 3,200ms with span attributes showing thousands of rows returned. What is the most likely root cause and fix? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The organization has exceeded its daily API call limit, so duration will only normalize once the daily limit resets at midnight."), ("B", "The LLM reasoning step before the action is misconfigured, so lowering the model's temperature setting will proportionally reduce the action's execution duration."), ("C", "The backing query lacks a selective WHERE filter and LIMIT clause, so it returns far more data than needed; adding both should sharply reduce duration."), ("D", "The action is being executed by an external MCP server, so nothing about its internal duration can be improved without vendor involvement.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A high row count from an unfiltered query is a well-documented cause of elevated duration; adding filtering/limits is the proven fix. A is incorrect: API limit exhaustion produces distinct governor errors, not gradually elevated duration. B is incorrect: Temperature affects response variability, not a separate backend query's execution time. D is incorrect: This describes native database attributes, not the opaque MCP HTTP boundary pattern."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "What is the primary benefit of joining session-level tracing data with service-level platform tracing data for a specific interaction? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "It automatically increases the accuracy of the agent's safety score by cross-referencing decisions against back-end execution timing data."), ("B", "It is required before any Consumption Analytics Dashboard panel can display token usage data for a given agent."), ("C", "It permanently deletes redundant span records, reducing Data Cloud storage costs by removing duplicate entries between the two data sources."), ("D", "It connects the conversational context of what the agent decided with the back-end execution detail of why a specific step was slow or broke.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Session tracing shows decisions while platform tracing shows execution; joining them gives complete visibility. A is incorrect: Safety scoring is computed independently by the trust layer. B is incorrect: Consumption dashboards read from usage tables independently. C is incorrect: Joining doesn't involve deleting records; the sources capture different granularities."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A non-technical support manager wants to ask, in plain language, why a specific interaction took several seconds, without writing any queries themselves. What capability supports this workflow? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The Agentforce Analytics Dashboard's Topic Breakdown panel, which supports natural language filtering questions specifically about execution duration and span attributes."), ("B", "The Einstein Trust Layer audit trail, which automatically converts every stored prompt and response into a spoken-language latency summary for any manager who requests one."), ("C", "A conversational interface connected to an org with platform tracing enabled, provided with sample query patterns so it can translate natural language questions into trace lookups."), ("D", "The Consumption Insights Dashboard, which requires no setup and answers free-form latency questions directly without any underlying schema configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A conversational assistant with schema and query patterns can translate plain-language latency questions into structured trace lookups. A is incorrect: The topic breakdown panel shows invocation volume, not span-level filtering. B is incorrect: The audit trail logs prompts/responses for compliance, not latency explanations. D is incorrect: Consumption dashboards report token/credit usage, not free-form latency root-cause."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "How does session-level trace observability typically change when an agent action is powered by an external MCP server rather than a native Apex or Flow action? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Grounding checks are automatically skipped for any response generated using data returned by an MCP action, regardless of instruction configuration."), ("B", "The FunctionStep for an MCP action always reports a duration of zero milliseconds, since MCP servers do not report timing information to the platform."), ("C", "The action invocation is still recorded, but the output detail available depends on the MCP server's own logging, since native database-level attributes aren't visible past the HTTP boundary."), ("D", "Session tracing becomes completely unavailable for the entire conversation turn once any single action in that turn uses an MCP server.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Invocation and latency are still recorded, but internal detail normally available for native actions is opaque past the HTTP boundary. A is incorrect: Grounding checks still run against whatever data the MCP action returns. B is incorrect: The recorded duration reflects the full round-trip time, not zero. D is incorrect: Only detail for that specific external action is limited; the rest of the turn's trace remains available."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "In a trace tree for an MCP-backed action, what pattern indicates that platform tracing has reached the external HTTP boundary and cannot see further? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "A status code of OK on the parent span combined with a status code of ERROR on every one of its child spans without exception."), ("B", "The absence of child spans with native database attributes underneath the external service invocation span, unlike a Flow action which would show nested steps with query-level detail."), ("C", "A span duration that is always exactly double the duration of the equivalent native Apex action performing the same logical operation."), ("D", "A missing operation name field on the parent span, since external actions are the only span type that omits this field entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Missing nested detail where native actions would show it signals the trace has reached the boundary of what's observable. A is incorrect: Status combinations don't reliably indicate an HTTP boundary. C is incorrect: There's no fixed duration multiplier distinguishing MCP calls. D is incorrect: Operation name fields are still populated for external actions."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "A voice-channel agent chains two sequential MCP callouts per turn, each averaging 400ms round-trip. What architectural concern should this raise for a Success Architect? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Voice channels do not support any external HTTP-based actions, so the design must be rebuilt entirely using only native Apex actions before launch."), ("B", "Chaining two MCP callouts always doubles the credit cost of the interaction compared to a single callout, regardless of each call's actual duration."), ("C", "The compounded latency at higher percentiles could easily exceed the sub-two-second response threshold considered important for a good voice-channel experience."), ("D", "Sequential MCP callouts always violate platform governor limits regardless of their individual duration, so the design will be blocked at deployment time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Chained external calls compound at higher percentiles, easily pushing p95 latency past the sub-two-second voice target. A is incorrect: Voice channels can invoke external actions; the concern is compounded response time. B is incorrect: Credit costs are tied to per-action execution, not automatically doubled. D is incorrect: Sequential external calls aren't automatically blocked by governor limits."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "Which out-of-the-box dashboard panel is most directly tied to tracking adoption momentum in the weeks following an agent's launch? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Conversation Volume, since daily and weekly session counts directly validate whether awareness and usage of the new channel are growing."), ("B", "Sub-agent Invocation Count, since counting invocations per subagent is specifically designed to validate whether awareness of the agent channel is increasing."), ("C", "Safety Score Distribution, since a rising distribution of safety scores over time is the standard proxy used to measure early user adoption."), ("D", "Token Usage by User, since identifying the heaviest individual token consumers is the primary signal used to measure adoption momentum.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Tracking session counts over time is the standard way to validate channel adoption. B is incorrect: Invocation counts reveal routing distribution, distinct from overall adoption. C is incorrect: Safety score distribution reflects quality/governance, not adoption. D is incorrect: Token consumption identifies power users, not overall adoption."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "A team managing Einstein credit budgets across multiple AI products wants granular per-feature and per-model token breakdowns beyond what the standard consumption dashboard offers. Which reporting surface is designed for this need? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The Consumption Insights Dashboard, which provides more granular consumption data including per-feature and per-model breakdowns for managing credit budgets."), ("B", "The Einstein Trust Layer Audit Trail DMOs, which report token consumption per feature once audit trail data streams are enabled."), ("C", "The Agentforce Analytics Dashboard, which is specifically designed to break down credit consumption by feature and model across multiple AI products."), ("D", "The Sub-agent Invocation Count panel, which includes an embedded per-model credit consumption breakdown alongside invocation totals.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This dashboard is specifically built for granular per-feature/per-model consumption detail. B is incorrect: Audit trail DMOs capture prompts/responses/feedback, not consumption reporting. C is incorrect: This dashboard focuses on usage, escalation, and deflection metrics for a single agent. D is incorrect: Invocation count panels have no built-in credit breakdown by model."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "Which Data Cloud table would be queried to find the raw text of what users actually typed to the agent, as opposed to the compiled prompt sent to the LLM? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The interaction message table, filtered to the role representing user-authored messages."), ("B", "The generation metadata table, which stores per-call model configuration rather than message content."), ("C", "The feedback table, which stores thumbs-up/thumbs-down ratings rather than message text."), ("D", "The gateway request table, which captures the compiled prompt exactly as delivered to the LLM.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This table stores user and agent messages; filtering to user role retrieves the literal utterance text. B is incorrect: This stores generation metadata, not message content. C is incorrect: This stores feedback ratings, not typed message text. D is incorrect: This captures the fully compiled prompt including instructions and history, not just raw utterance."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A team wants to build a custom dashboard panel that flags sessions where a user appears to be \"stuck,\" repeating the same message multiple times without progress. Which table and pattern supports this analysis? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The session participant table, filtered for sessions involving more than two distinct subagents during a single conversation."), ("B", "The interaction message table, filtered for sessions where an identical user message appears more than twice within the same session."), ("C", "The generative AI usage table, filtered for sessions where token consumption exceeds the organization's daily budget threshold."), ("D", "The telemetry span table, filtered for spans where the status code is OK and the duration exceeds two seconds.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Counting repeated identical user messages directly surfaces the \"stuck\" pattern. A is incorrect: Multi-subagent involvement reflects routing complexity, not user repetition. C is incorrect: Token consumption reflects cost, not whether a user is stuck repeating a message. D is incorrect: Span duration/status reflect backend performance, not user-side repetition."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Which proactive monitoring alert would fire specifically because of a completely failed initialization of an agent conversation, representing a full outage for affected users? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Planner session start failures"), ("B", "LLM model latency threshold breach"), ("C", "Voice agent response failures"), ("D", "Agent rate limit errors")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A failure at session initialization means the conversation can't begin at all, a complete outage for affected users. B is incorrect: A latency breach means responses are slow, not that the session never started. C is incorrect: This alert is specific to voice channel TTS/STT pipeline errors. D is incorrect: Rate limit errors reflect capacity exhaustion causing delays, not a complete inability to start."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "A team wants to calculate cost avoidance from an agent deployment for a leadership presentation. Which calculation best reflects a defensible methodology? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Multiply total session count by the average credit cost per session, regardless of whether each session escalated to a human agent."), ("B", "Multiply total escalated sessions by the human cost per interaction, since escalated sessions represent the true cost savings delivered by the agent."), ("C", "Multiply token usage per session by the number of active users, since token volume directly represents the dollar value of cost avoidance."), ("D", "Multiply the count of genuine deflections by the difference between the human cost per interaction and the AI credit cost per session.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Cost avoidance should be based on genuinely resolved sessions multiplied by net savings versus human-handled cost. A is incorrect: This counts total AI cost without netting against savings and includes escalated sessions. B is incorrect: Escalated sessions still required human handling, so they represent no cost avoidance. C is incorrect: Token usage reflects consumption cost, not avoided interaction value."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A quality-corrected deflection analysis finds that 14% of \"deflected\" sessions actually had error spans with zero rows returned. What should the team conclude and do next? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "These sessions should be immediately excluded from all future dashboard reporting entirely, since zero-row spans are always evidence of a corrupted trace record."), ("B", "These sessions indicate the agent's safety score calculation is broken, since safety scoring and zero-row query results are directly linked by design."), ("C", "These sessions should be reclassified as escalations retroactively in the historical data, since any zero-row span technically qualifies as a failed escalation."), ("D", "These sessions likely represent graceful failures rather than genuine resolutions, so fixing the underlying data issue could meaningfully increase the true deflection rate.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A zero-row result often means the agent had nothing to work with, pointing to a concrete fix opportunity. A is incorrect: Zero-row spans are a valid, expected occurrence, not corruption. B is incorrect: Safety scoring is unrelated to whether a query returned rows. C is incorrect: Retroactive reclassification changes definitions rather than addressing the cause."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "Which credit consumption behavior is accurate for standard Agentforce actions under the platform's cost model? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Prompt Templates are always free regardless of invocation count, since only Flow and Apex actions carry any credit cost under the platform's model."), ("B", "Credit costs apply only in production orgs, so sandbox testing with live preview mode never consumes any credits regardless of configuration."), ("C", "Deterministic framework operations like variable-setting and control flow are free, while actions such as Flow, Apex, and most other invoked actions carry a per-execution credit cost."), ("D", "Every LLM reasoning turn carries the same fixed credit cost as a Flow action, since both consume equivalent processing resources per invocation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Framework-level operations resolve deterministically without an LLM call and are free, while invoked actions carry a credit cost. A is incorrect: Prompt Templates carry a credit cost per invocation. B is incorrect: Live preview mode in sandbox still incurs the same credit costs as production. D is incorrect: LLM reasoning turns are free while the resulting action call is not."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "Why does using simulated preview mode for iterative instruction and routing testing represent a cost-aware best practice? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Because it automatically converts all Apex actions in the agent into free framework operations for the duration of the testing session."), ("B", "Because it reduces the safety score threshold required for a response to be delivered, lowering the number of retry-driven credit charges."), ("C", "Because it avoids the credit costs of real Flow and Apex action executions while still validating routing and instruction logic during iteration."), ("D", "Because it permanently disables credit billing for the entire org for as long as any agent remains in simulated mode configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Fake action outputs don't trigger real execution, avoiding per-execution credit costs during iterative testing. A is incorrect: Simulated mode doesn't reclassify action types; it generates placeholder outputs. B is incorrect: Safety score thresholds are unrelated to preview mode selection. D is incorrect: Simulated mode is scoped to test sessions, not an org-wide billing change."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "A customer asks whether native support exists today for automatically detecting cross-subagent contradictions, such as one subagent saying a refund was approved while another later says it's pending. What is the most accurate response? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "This capability is impossible to achieve in any form, since no single trace ever contains both subagents' statements for comparison."), ("B", "This kind of semantic conflict detection is currently on the product roadmap; today it must be approximated using custom queries against session-tracing data with manual pattern detection."), ("C", "This capability has been fully available as a native, no-configuration feature since the introduction of the Einstein Trust Layer's fail-safe routing system."), ("D", "This capability is available today exclusively through the Consumption Analytics Dashboard's built-in cross-subagent contradiction detection panel.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Native semantic conflict detection is a roadmap item; today it requires manual custom queries. A is incorrect: Cross-session analysis can surface it, so it isn't impossible, just not yet native. C is incorrect: Trust Layer fail-safe routing addresses harmful responses, not cross-subagent contradictions. D is incorrect: The consumption dashboard reports token/credit usage, not contradiction detection."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "Which item is accurately described as currently available rather than an item still on the product roadmap? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Native sentiment analysis inferred automatically from session message content."), ("B", "A safety score attached to every agent response via the platform response step."), ("C", "Native latency split tracking showing orchestration overhead separated from subagent execution time."), ("D", "Native error rate metric splitting by category such as orchestration, handoff, auth, and timeout failures.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Every agent response already carries a safety score as part of the standard response step output. A is incorrect: Sentiment analysis is not currently available natively. C is incorrect: Component-level latency splitting is a roadmap item requiring manual extraction today. D is incorrect: Category-level error splitting is a roadmap item; today requires manual categorization."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "Why should Success Architects frame roadmap items carefully in stakeholder conversations rather than promising a specific delivery date? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Because roadmap items are legally required to be withheld from any customer conversation until they are formally released to general availability."), ("B", "Because customers are contractually barred from requesting any roadmap feature be prioritized ahead of its listed general availability window."), ("C", "Because roadmap items are described as future capabilities with manual workarounds available today, not committed features with guaranteed timelines."), ("D", "Because mentioning roadmap items in any capacity voids the current production support agreement for observability tooling.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Framing roadmap items as directional rather than committed sets accurate expectations without overpromising. A is incorrect: There's no blanket restriction against discussing roadmap direction with customers. B is incorrect: Customers can typically provide feedback or prioritization input. D is incorrect: Discussing roadmap features doesn't affect support agreements."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "Before promoting an agent from sandbox to production, which practice best ensures observability continuity at go-live? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Rely exclusively on local CLI trace files generated during sandbox testing, since these files remain valid indefinitely for diagnosing production issues."), ("B", "Disable platform tracing in production until 30 days after go-live, since enabling it too early is known to corrupt the first month of session data."), ("C", "Assume platform tracing inherits automatically from sandbox settings, since sandbox and production share a single unified tracing configuration by default."), ("D", "Verify platform tracing is enabled in the production org specifically, since this configuration does not automatically carry over from sandbox and must be confirmed separately.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Platform tracing must be enabled per org and doesn't inherit from another environment. A is incorrect: Local CLI trace files are ephemeral and tied to sandbox preview sessions. B is incorrect: There's no mechanism by which early enablement corrupts data. C is incorrect: Tracing configuration is scoped per org; sandbox and production don't share a single toggle."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "Why should alert thresholds be configured relative to a customer's expected production volume rather than left at generic defaults? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Because a low-volume deployment could exceed a percentage-based anomaly threshold at a much lower absolute count than a high-volume org, so defaults calibrated for high volume may fail to alert appropriately."), ("B", "Because alert thresholds are the only configuration element that determines whether platform tracing captures any data at all in production."), ("C", "Because generic default thresholds are hard-coded to permanently disable all alerting for any org with fewer than one million monthly sessions."), ("D", "Because expected volume directly determines the org's Data Cloud storage retention window, which must be set before any alerts can be configured.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Defaults are often designed for high-volume orgs, so smaller deployments need thresholds calibrated to their own traffic. B is incorrect: Alert thresholds control notifications, not data capture. C is incorrect: There's no volume-based mechanism that disables alerting entirely. D is incorrect: Retention windows are a separate Data Cloud configuration concern."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "A team promoting an agent to production plans to keep using individual local trace file review as their primary diagnostic method at scale. What is the concern with this approach? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Local trace files contain more information than the aggregate query tables, so this remains the recommended approach even at very high production volumes."), ("B", "Manually opening individual trace files does not scale to production volumes, and the team should transition to aggregate SOQL queries against the tracing tables before go-live."), ("C", "Local trace files are automatically deleted the moment an agent is promoted to production, so this workflow will fail immediately regardless of scale."), ("D", "Local trace file review is forbidden by platform policy in any environment once an agent has completed its first production deployment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Manual per-file review becomes impractical at high production volume, making aggregate SOQL the necessary workflow. A is incorrect: Aggregate tables are recommended for scale precisely because manual review doesn't hold up. C is incorrect: Trace file deletion isn't automatically triggered by promotion. D is incorrect: There's no described policy forbidding trace file review."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "A team wants to deploy the setting that enables service-level platform tracing consistently across environments as part of their release pipeline, rather than relying on manual Setup UI toggles. What approach best supports this? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Package the relevant custom metadata type with the enablement flag set to true and deploy it in the same pipeline stage as the agent's other metadata."), ("B", "Request that Salesforce Support toggle the setting on the customer's behalf before every release, since it is not configurable by customer admins."), ("C", "Manually toggle the setting in each sandbox and production org separately after every single deployment, since this setting cannot be captured in any metadata type."), ("D", "Use a scheduled Flow that runs nightly to check and re-enable the setting, since metadata deployment cannot reliably set toggle-based settings.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This setting is exposed via custom metadata and can be deployed as part of a standard metadata package. B is incorrect: The setting is customer-configurable and doesn't require Support intervention. C is incorrect: Manual toggling is exactly the error-prone process metadata packaging eliminates. D is incorrect: A scheduled Flow is unnecessary when the setting can be captured directly in metadata."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "What is the key difference in how utterance diversity should be treated between sandbox testing and production monitoring? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Sandbox utterances are controlled test cases representing expected patterns, while production utterances reflect the full, uncontrolled diversity of real user phrasing."), ("B", "There is no meaningful difference, since sandbox test scripts are required to exactly replicate the full diversity of real production traffic before go-live."), ("C", "Sandbox utterances always exceed production utterances in overall volume, since sandbox testing requires simulating significantly more traffic than production receives."), ("D", "Production utterances must be manually pre-approved by an administrator before being processed, unlike sandbox utterances which run automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Test utterances represent expected use cases, while production traffic includes unpredictable real phrasing no test suite can fully anticipate. B is incorrect: The described gap is exactly why observability monitoring matters after launch. C is incorrect: Sandbox testing typically involves far lower volume than production. D is incorrect: There's no manual pre-approval gate for production utterances."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "Which scenario best illustrates the value of translating a technical metric into a business KPI for non-technical stakeholders? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Converting a session ID into its corresponding trace ID for use in a subsequent SOQL query against the telemetry span table."), ("B", "Converting genuine deflection counts and cost-per-interaction figures into a monthly dollar figure representing cost avoidance for a service operations leader."), ("C", "Converting a span's operation name into its corresponding Apex class name for a developer debugging a specific action failure."), ("D", "Converting a safety score threshold from a decimal value into a percentage value for display on an internal engineering dashboard.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Translating a technical measure into a business outcome is the core purpose of KPI mapping for leadership. A is incorrect: This supports further technical querying, not stakeholder communication. C is incorrect: This is a technical debugging step, not a business-facing translation. D is incorrect: Reformatting a number for display isn't translating a metric into a business outcome."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "Why is latency on a voice-channel agent considered a cost driver and not just a performance nuisance? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Excess latency is billed separately as its own line item on the monthly invoice, independent of any credit consumption from actions or Prompt Templates."), ("B", "Excess latency increases the risk of user abandonment or escalation, and escalated interactions typically cost significantly more than deflected ones, so latency reduction has a direct dollar impact."), ("C", "Excess latency directly increases the per-session Salesforce license fee charged to the organization for every additional second of response time."), ("D", "Excess latency automatically triggers a mandatory downgrade of the org's Data Cloud storage tier until the issue is resolved.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Slow responses drive abandonment/escalation, and escalated interactions cost substantially more than deflections. A is incorrect: Latency isn't a separate billed line item; credit costs tie to action/template invocations. C is incorrect: Licensing fees aren't structured around per-second response latency. D is incorrect: There's no mechanism linking latency to storage tier changes."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "A Success Architect wants to quantify whether reducing average interaction duration actually correlates with lower escalation rates for a specific customer. What is the correct analytical approach? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Identify sessions above a latency threshold, cross-reference them against escalation status, and calculate what percentage of above-threshold sessions escalated versus resolved."), ("B", "Count the total number of subagents configured in the agent, since subagent count is the primary driver of any correlation between latency and escalation."), ("C", "Review the Consumption Insights Dashboard's per-model token breakdown, since token breakdown by model directly determines escalation likelihood for any given session."), ("D", "Compare the customer's safety score distribution against a same-industry benchmark published externally, since safety score is a direct proxy for latency-driven escalation correlation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Segmenting sessions by duration threshold and checking escalation outcome is the direct evidence-based correlation test. B is incorrect: Subagent count reflects architectural complexity, not a causal driver here. C is incorrect: Token breakdown reflects cost attribution, not this correlation. D is incorrect: Safety score isn't a valid proxy for latency-escalation correlation."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "Which distractor-avoidance concept from a subagent routing failure would be addressed by adding \"exclusion language\" to overlapping subagent descriptions? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Exclusion language refers to removing all descriptive text from a subagent so the LLM defaults to the most recently added subagent in the list."), ("B", "Exclusion language refers to a required legal disclaimer inserted into every subagent's response to satisfy compliance review before go-live."), ("C", "Exclusion language refers to blocking certain user demographics from being routed to a specific subagent based on their account profile fields."), ("D", "When two subagents' descriptions both plausibly match a given topic, adding language that clarifies what each subagent explicitly does NOT handle reduces routing ambiguity.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Explicitly stating what a subagent does not cover helps the routing LLM disambiguate overlapping topics. A is incorrect: Removing text entirely makes routing less informed, not more precise. B is incorrect: This describes a compliance disclaimer, unrelated to routing ambiguity. C is incorrect: This describes access restriction, not clarifying topic overlap."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "An action is correctly listed as available to the LLM in the relevant trace step, but the agent never actually invokes it despite users asking for it in a way that should trigger it. What is the most likely fix? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Disable grounding checks for that specific action, since grounding checks are the most common reason a visible action is never invoked."), ("B", "Remove the action from the agent entirely, since an action that's visible but unused should always be deleted rather than revised."), ("C", "Strengthen the action's description with trigger keywords derived from how real users actually phrase their requests, since the current description doesn't match natural phrasing closely enough."), ("D", "Increase the model's temperature setting, since higher temperature settings are the standard fix for an agent failing to invoke a visible, available action.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "When visible but unchosen, the mismatch is typically between the description and real user phrasing. A is incorrect: Grounding runs after execution and doesn't determine invocation choice. B is incorrect: Deleting a working but underused action discards functionality needing only description tuning. D is incorrect: Temperature affects variability broadly, not description-phrasing match."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "Which fix is most appropriate when a behavioral loop is caused by an instruction that checks an intermediate variable rather than conversation history, causing the agent to repeatedly ask a question the user already answered? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Reduce the number of options presented in the agent's multi-select actions, since fewer options are known to prevent variable-based behavioral loops."), ("B", "Increase the safety score threshold required before any response is delivered to the user, since higher thresholds are the standard fix for instruction-driven behavioral loops."), ("C", "Rewrite the instruction to treat conversation history as the source of truth and slot-fill from prior turns rather than relying solely on the variable's current state."), ("D", "Disable the subagent responsible for the loop entirely and route all future conversations exclusively through the main router subagent instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Shifting the check to conversation history resolves the loop at its root cause. A is incorrect: Option count is unrelated to re-asking an already-answered question. B is incorrect: Safety score thresholds govern harmfulness, not repetition logic. D is incorrect: Disabling a subagent removes functionality rather than fixing the instruction logic."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "A response was blocked, and consecutive grounding checks on retried responses were also rejected, resulting in a generic apology message to the user. What does this pattern typically indicate? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The org has exceeded its monthly token allocation, which forces every subsequent response for the rest of the billing cycle to fail grounding automatically."), ("B", "Two consecutive ungrounded results triggered the terminal fallback, likely because the agent embellished or paraphrased too loosely, or the backing action itself returned an error with no usable data."), ("C", "The agent's safety score exceeded the maximum allowed value, which automatically converts any response into a generic apology regardless of grounding status."), ("D", "The user's account was automatically flagged for security review, which is the only condition that can trigger two consecutive grounding failures in a row.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A terminal apology appears after two consecutive ungrounded results, commonly from embellishment or unusable action data. A is incorrect: Token exhaustion produces a different kind of platform error. C is incorrect: There's no mechanism where exceeding a safety score triggers this fallback. D is incorrect: Security flags aren't described as a grounding mechanism."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "When an action returns valid, successful data but the agent's reply is generic and doesn't reference any of it, where should the investigation focus first? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The permission set assigned to the runtime agent user, since permission set misconfiguration is the only known cause of a generic reply following a successful action."), ("B", "The reasoning step immediately following the successful action execution, checking whether the LLM's response actually incorporated the returned data or ignored it in favor of a generic reply."), ("C", "The routing step that occurred several turns earlier, since a generic reply always traces back exclusively to the original subagent handoff decision."), ("D", "The initial state snapshot captured before the conversation even began, since generic replies are always caused by a stale variable set before the session started.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Checking the decision step right after the action reveals whether returned data was actually used. A is incorrect: Permission issues would typically prevent successful execution altogether. C is incorrect: An earlier routing decision doesn't directly explain ignoring later action data. D is incorrect: Session-start state doesn't explain why this specific successful action's output was ignored."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "Which fix best addresses a pattern where an agent's action successfully returns data, but the agent still composes a vague response instead of citing specific fields from that data? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Reduce the number of fields returned by the action to exactly one field, since agents cannot compose specific replies referencing more than one field."), ("B", "Convert the action from a Flow-based action to an Apex-based action, since Apex actions are inherently better at producing specific replies."), ("C", "Update the agent's instructions to explicitly name the specific output fields it should include when composing its reply."), ("D", "Increase the credit budget allocated to that specific action, since insufficient credits are the most common cause of vague post-action responses.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Explicitly naming fields gives the agent clear guidance on what to include, directly fixing vague replies. A is incorrect: Agents can reference multiple fields; limiting to one isn't a recognized fix. B is incorrect: Implementation type doesn't inherently determine reply specificity. D is incorrect: Credit budget affects execution ability, not reply specificity."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "Which infrastructure readiness check should be completed before any other observability configuration begins for a new org? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Confirming Data Cloud is provisioned and active, since every other tracing and analytics feature depends on this foundational layer being in place."), ("B", "Confirming the agent's safety score threshold is set to exactly 0.95, since this is a universal prerequisite required before Data Cloud can be provisioned."), ("C", "Confirming that Voice Agent functionality is disabled, since voice channels are incompatible with any Data Cloud-backed observability feature."), ("D", "Confirming the org's API version is set to the newest possible release, since older API versions block all Data Cloud DMO queries regardless of provisioning.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Both session and platform tracing store data in Data Cloud DMOs, making this the necessary first step. B is incorrect: Safety score thresholds aren't a prerequisite for provisioning Data Cloud. C is incorrect: Voice channels aren't described as incompatible with Data Cloud observability. D is incorrect: API version isn't described as a blocking prerequisite here."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "Two permission sets are described as core administrative grants for managing Data Cloud setup and accessing Agentforce Studio authoring tools respectively. Which pairing correctly matches permission to purpose? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "GenieAdmin grants access to Agentforce Studio and agent authoring APIs, while CopilotSalesforceAdmin grants Data Cloud administrative access."), ("B", "GenieAdmin grants Data Cloud administrative access, while CopilotSalesforceAdmin grants access to Agentforce Studio and agent authoring APIs."), ("C", "Both GenieAdmin and CopilotSalesforceAdmin grant identical access to Data Cloud administrative functions with no distinction between them."), ("D", "Both GenieAdmin and CopilotSalesforceAdmin exclusively grant runtime execution access for service agents rather than any administrative capability.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "One permission is for Data Cloud administration, the other for Studio/authoring capability. A is incorrect: This reverses the correct mapping. C is incorrect: These two permissions serve distinct purposes and aren't identical. D is incorrect: Runtime execution access is granted by a separate permission entirely."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "A knowledge-grounded agent using a retrieval mechanism needs its runtime user to be able to query Data Cloud for grounding content. Which permission best supports this specific runtime need? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "GenieAdmin"), ("B", "CopilotSalesforceAdmin"), ("C", "AgentforceServiceAgentUser alone, with no additional Data Cloud permission required"), ("D", "GenieUserEnhancedSecurity or DataCloudUser")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "These permissions specifically grant Data Cloud query access needed for retrieval-augmented grounding. A is incorrect: This permission is scoped to Data Cloud administrative setup, not runtime query access. B is incorrect: This permission is for human authoring/Studio access, not runtime Data Cloud querying. C is incorrect: This runtime identity alone doesn't include Data Cloud query access."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "Which statement accurately reflects a best practice for combining out-of-the-box dashboards with custom Data Cloud queries in an observability strategy? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Out-of-the-box dashboards must be fully disabled before any custom query against the underlying data tables can return accurate results."), ("B", "Custom queries should always be avoided in favor of out-of-the-box dashboards, since dashboards contain strictly more detail than any query could ever surface."), ("C", "Custom queries and out-of-the-box dashboards read from entirely unrelated, non-overlapping data sources with no shared underlying tables."), ("D", "Out-of-the-box dashboards are the starting point for surfacing broad trends, while custom queries against the underlying data tables are typically needed to answer more specific, non-standard questions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Standard dashboards suit broad questions, but specific diagnostic questions typically require custom queries against underlying tables. A is incorrect: There's no requirement to disable dashboards before running custom queries. B is incorrect: Dashboards can't surface arbitrary custom patterns, which is why custom queries remain necessary. C is incorrect: Custom queries and dashboards typically draw from the same or related underlying tables."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "A team wants to distinguish network latency from external server processing time for an MCP-backed action, but the MCP server has no instrumentation of its own. What is the most accurate conclusion they can draw from platform tracing data alone? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "The recorded span duration always reflects pure external server processing time with zero contribution from network latency under any circumstance."), ("B", "The recorded span duration cannot be trusted at all and should be discarded until the MCP server implements its own logging framework."), ("C", "The recorded span duration always reflects pure network latency with zero contribution from external server processing time under any circumstance."), ("D", "The recorded span duration represents an upper bound on total round-trip time, but without server-side instrumentation, network latency cannot be cleanly separated from external processing time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Without server-side instrumentation, the recorded duration is the best available figure but only an upper bound, not a clean breakdown. A is incorrect: The recorded duration includes network latency in addition to server processing. B is incorrect: The duration figure remains a valid upper-bound measurement, not something to discard. C is incorrect: The recorded duration includes both network transit and server processing combined."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "Which combination of evidence would most convincingly support a claim that reducing a specific action's latency directly reduced escalation rate for a customer? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "A decrease in the total number of subagents configured in the agent, since subagent count reduction is independently correlated with escalation rate in all deployments."), ("B", "A single anecdotal report from one support agent stating that the agent \"seems faster\" since the most recent deployment, with no further data reviewed."), ("C", "An increase in daily active users following the latency fix, since user growth alone is sufficient evidence of a causal link to escalation rate."), ("D", "A measured drop in average duration for that action's spans occurring in the same period as a measured drop in the percentage of above-threshold sessions that escalate.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Pairing measured duration improvement with a corresponding drop in escalation rate is the direct, data-backed causal evidence. A is incorrect: Subagent count is an architectural detail not established as correlated with escalation rate. B is incorrect: A single anecdotal impression isn't measurable evidence of a quantified relationship. C is incorrect: DAU growth reflects adoption, not a response-time correlation with escalation."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "An Agentforce action guard requires a variable to be set before the action becomes visible to the LLM, but the variable is populated by an instruction in a different subagent that gets bypassed in certain conversation paths. What trace evidence would confirm this as the root cause of the action never firing? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The EnabledToolsStep for the relevant reasoning cycle does not list the action, confirming it was structurally invisible to the LLM."), ("B", "The NodeEntryStateStep for the subagent shows the guard variable is empty or unset at the point the action would have been needed."), ("C", "The ReasoningStep for a previous turn shows a GROUNDED result for an unrelated response."), ("D", "The PlannerResponseStep shows a safety score above 0.9 for the final delivered response in that turn."), ("E", "The FunctionStep for an unrelated action in the same turn shows a successful execution with normal latency.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Together, these confirm the action was excluded because its guard condition wasn't met, and that the variable never received a value before it mattered — pinpointing the exact structural cause. C is incorrect: A grounded result on a prior, unrelated response doesn't address the guard-variable dependency causing this action to stay hidden. D is incorrect: A high safety score reflects response harmfulness assessment and has no bearing on why a guarded action was excluded from tool visibility. E is incorrect: A successful unrelated action confirms that action's own execution but says nothing about why the guarded action's variable was never set."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A Success Architect is building a custom ROI narrative for a customer and needs to combine data from multiple STDM tables. Which two tables would need to be joined to calculate a per-session credit cost alongside that session's escalation outcome? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "ssot__TelemetryTraceSpan__dlm, which records back-end execution span durations."), ("B", "GenAIFeedback__dlm, which records thumbs-up/thumbs-down ratings on responses."), ("C", "ssot__AiAgentSession__dlm, which records escalation status per session."), ("D", "AiAgentGenerativeAiUsage_std__dlm, which records token/credit usage per session.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Escalation status lives on the session table while credit/token consumption lives on the usage table; joining them on session identifiers ties cost directly to outcome. A is incorrect: Span durations describe back-end execution timing, not the credit cost or escalation outcome of the session as a whole. B is incorrect: Feedback ratings capture user sentiment, not session-level escalation status or credit cost."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "A Success Architect is troubleshooting a customer's claim that an agent 'never uses the data it looks up.' Which two trace elements together would confirm whether the agent's final reply actually incorporated returned action data? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The EnabledToolsStep showing which actions were visible three turns earlier."), ("B", "The SessionInitialStateStep from the very first turn of the session."), ("C", "The FunctionStep showing the action executed successfully with real returned data."), ("D", "The LLMStep immediately following that FunctionStep, showing whether the response_messages reference the returned values.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Confirming the action actually returned usable data, and then checking whether the very next LLM decision step referenced that data in its response, is the direct evidence chain for whether returned data was used or ignored. A is incorrect: Tool visibility from several turns earlier doesn't establish whether the current turn's action output was incorporated into the reply. B is incorrect: The first turn's initial state reflects session start conditions and has no bearing on whether a specific later action's output was used."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "Which two permission sets, when both missing from a runtime agent user, would most directly block a knowledge-grounded agent from successfully retrieving grounding content from Data Cloud? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "GenieAdmin"), ("B", "GenieUserEnhancedSecurity"), ("C", "CopilotSalesforceAdmin"), ("D", "DataCloudUser")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "These are the two runtime Data Cloud query-access permission variants; a knowledge-grounded agent's runtime user needs one of them to query Data Cloud content for retrieval-augmented grounding, so missing both directly blocks retrieval. A is incorrect: This is a Data Cloud administrative setup permission (data streams, retrievers, setup), not a runtime query-access grant for the agent user itself. C is incorrect: This is a human authoring/Studio permission for building and publishing agents, not a runtime query-access grant."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "A customer's escalation rate has stayed flat for months, but support tickets referencing the agent have quietly increased. Which two custom analyses would help explain this discrepancy? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Cross-referencing 'deflected' sessions against error spans with zero rows returned, to check for graceful-failure sessions hiding inside the deflection count."), ("B", "Increasing the safety score threshold required before a response can be delivered to any user."), ("C", "Disabling Agent Platform Tracing to reduce the volume of span data being written to Data Cloud."), ("D", "Building a 'stuck session' query that flags sessions where the same user message repeats more than twice without progress.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Both analyses surface hidden failure patterns that a flat aggregate escalation rate can mask — graceful failures counted as deflections, and users stuck repeating themselves without ever escalating. B is incorrect: Raising the safety threshold affects response delivery based on harmfulness, not detection of hidden failure patterns behind stagnant escalation numbers. C is incorrect: Disabling tracing would remove the very data needed to investigate this discrepancy, not help explain it."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "Which two conditions must both be true for a Success Architect to reliably reconstruct the full back-end execution tree for a specific customer-reported slow interaction? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Agent Platform Tracing was enabled in that org before the interaction occurred."), ("B", "The customer's org has purchased a separate consumption analytics add-on license."), ("C", "The interaction's TelemetryTraceId is known so spans can be filtered to that specific trace."), ("D", "The agent's safety score for that interaction was below the configured threshold.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Without tracing enabled beforehand, no spans exist to reconstruct; without the specific trace ID, spans can't be isolated to the interaction in question — both are required together. B is incorrect: There's no described separate paid add-on requirement for accessing platform tracing span data. D is incorrect: Safety score has no bearing on whether execution spans exist or can be retrieved for a given interaction."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "Which three metrics are described as key indicators tracked on the standard Agentforce Analytics Dashboard for gauging early post-launch agent health? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Escalation Rate"), ("B", "Deflection Rate"), ("C", "Apex CPU Time per Transaction"), ("D", "Conversation Volume"), ("E", "Sandbox Refresh Frequency")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 3], explanation: "Escalation rate, deflection rate, and conversation volume are all standard panels on the out-of-the-box analytics dashboard used to gauge adoption and quality in the weeks after launch. C is incorrect: Apex CPU time is a platform governor-limit metric tracked at the code execution level, not a panel on the Agentforce Analytics Dashboard. E is incorrect: Sandbox refresh frequency is an org-management concern unrelated to agent usage or quality analytics."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "Which three items are accurately described as currently on the observability product roadmap rather than natively available today? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Session-level trace files generated via the CLI preview workflow."), ("B", "Native error rate metric splitting by category (orchestration, handoff, auth, timeout)."), ("C", "Native latency split tracking separating orchestration overhead from subagent execution time."), ("D", "A safety score attached to every agent response."), ("E", "Native sentiment analysis inferred automatically from session message content.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2, 4], explanation: "Category-level error splitting, component-level latency splitting, and automatic sentiment inference are all called out as roadmap items requiring manual workarounds today. A is incorrect: CLI-generated session trace files are already a currently available part of the sandbox preview workflow, not a future capability. D is incorrect: Every agent response already carries a safety score today as part of the standard response step output — this is currently available, not a roadmap item."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "Which three of the following are legitimate root causes of a grounding check failing (marked UNGROUNDED) on an otherwise successful action response? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "The agent rounded a returned numeric value instead of quoting it verbatim."), ("B", "The subagent's description overlapped with another subagent's description for the same topic."), ("C", "The agent added descriptive embellishment not present in the actual action output."), ("D", "The action's `available when` guard condition referenced an unset variable."), ("E", "The agent inferred a relative date reference instead of stating the exact date value returned.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2, 4], explanation: "Rounding, embellishment, and loose date inference are all specifically called out as common causes of grounding checks failing when comparing a response against actual action output. B is incorrect: Overlapping subagent descriptions cause routing ambiguity, which is a different failure mode from grounding a response against action output. D is incorrect: An unset guard variable would prevent the action from being visible/invoked in the first place — a routing/visibility issue, not a grounding comparison failure on a delivered response."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "Which three permission sets are each tied to a distinct administrative or runtime purpose in an Agentforce/Data Cloud observability setup, as opposed to being interchangeable with one another? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "GenieAdmin — Data Cloud administrative setup access."), ("B", "CopilotSalesforceAdmin — Agentforce Studio and agent authoring/publishing access."), ("C", "GenieUserEnhancedSecurity — grants administrative setup access to Data Cloud data streams."), ("D", "Data Cloud Data Access — grants agent authoring and publishing capability in Studio."), ("E", "AgentforceServiceAgentUser — runtime execution identity for service agents.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 4], explanation: "Each of these three permissions maps to a clearly distinct purpose — Data Cloud administration, agent authoring, and runtime service execution — and none of them substitute for each other. C is incorrect: This permission grants Data Cloud query access for knowledge-grounded runtime queries, not administrative setup access to data streams. D is incorrect: Data Cloud Data Access grants read access to DMOs for querying, not authoring or publishing capability in Studio.")
    ]
}
